Plugin callbacks keep the widget's context.

A callback's result event (an async command, file, http, stream, ...) carries
no snapshot, and the runtime started the callback with that empty snapshot, so
inside it `desktopWidget.outputName()` and the other per-instance bindings read
empty: the fleet monitor, which renders from its fetch callback, computed its
row width from a 0-wide output every few seconds. Callbacks now keep the
snapshot of the last call instead.

diff --git a/src/scripting/plugin_bindings.h b/src/scripting/plugin_bindings.h
index 30832d4..d617028 100644
--- a/src/scripting/plugin_bindings.h
+++ b/src/scripting/plugin_bindings.h
@@ -25,6 +25,13 @@ namespace scripting {
       patch = {};
       sideEffects.clear();
     }
+    // A callback's result event (async command, file, http, stream, ...) carries no
+    // snapshot, so the callback keeps the last call's: outputName() and the like
+    // would otherwise read empty inside it.
+    void beginCallback() {
+      patch = {};
+      sideEffects.clear();
+    }
   };
 
   void registerPluginBindings(lua_State* L, PluginBindingContext* context);
